=(20\parallel30)+(25\parallel25)\] \[R_{TH}=R_{12}
=\frac{20\times30}{20+30}
+\frac{25\times25}{25+25}\] \[R_{TH}=R_{12}
=(12+12.5)\ \text{k}\Omega\] \[R_{TH}=R_{12}
=24.5\ \text{k}\Omega\] (B) \(24.5~\text{k}\Omega\) <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ol><li>Parallel combination of resistors decreases the equivalent resistance of the combination.</li><li>Series combination of resistors increases the equivalent resistance of the combination.</li></ol></div>

Using the principle of superposition, analyze the effect of each input separately and then add the outputs. <img src="img/b2f333a12c1ed7e0.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> <strong>Case 1:</strong> \(V_{1}=1\,\text{V},\;V_{2}=0\) The first op-amp acts as an inverting amplifier: \[V_{o1}=-\frac{R_f}{R_1}V_{1}
=-\frac{10\,\text{k}\Omega}{10\,\text{k}\Omega}\times1
=-1\,\text{V}\] The second op-amp is a non-inverting amplifier with gain \[1+\frac{R_f}{R_1}
=1+\frac{1\,\text{k}\Omega}{2\,\text{k}\Omega}
=\frac{3}{2}.\] Hence, \[V_{o1}'=-1\times\frac{3}{2}
=-1.5\,\text{V}.\] <strong>Case 2:</strong> \(V_{1}=0,\;V_{2}=2\,\text{V}\) The non-inverting terminal voltage of the first op-amp is \[V_{+}=\frac{10}{10+10}\times2=1\,\text{V}.\] Since the op-amp is ideal, \[V_{o2}=V_{+}\left(1+\frac{10}{10}\right)
=1\times2
=2\,\text{V}.\] Output of the second op-amp: \[V_{o2}'=2\times\frac{3}{2}=3\,\text{V}.\] By superposition, \[V_{0}=V_{o1}'+V_{o2}'
=-1.5+3
=1.5\,\text{V}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(b) }1.5\,\text{V}}\]

The input offset voltage behaves like a small voltage source at the input of the op-amp. Hence, the output offset is amplified by the noise gain of the inverting amplifier. \[V_{o(\text{offset})}
=
\left(1+\frac{R_f}{R_1}\right)V_{ios}\] Given, \[R_f=560\,\text{k}\Omega,\qquad
R_1=10\,\text{k}\Omega,\qquad
V_{ios}=5\,\text{mV}\] Therefore, \[V_{o(\text{offset})}
=
\left(1+\frac{560}{10}\right)\times5\,\text{mV}
=
57\times5\,\text{mV}
=
285\,\text{mV}\] \[\boxed{\text{(c) }285\,\text{mV}}\]

An ideal integrator requires a resistor in parallel with the feedback capacitor to compensate for the input offset current. Since no such resistor is present, the input offset current continuously charges the capacitor. The output due to the input signal is a triangular wave with \[V_{o(pp)}=\frac{V_{i(pp)}}{4fRC}
=\frac{1}{4(10^3)(10^6)(1\,\mu\text{F})}
=0.25\,\text{V}.\] However, the input offset current produces a constant charging current through the capacitor: \[\frac{dV_o}{dt}=\frac{i_{os}}{C}
=\frac{1\,\mu\text{A}}{1\,\mu\text{F}}
=1\,\text{V/s}.\] Thus, the capacitor voltage keeps drifting in one direction. After a short time, the op-amp output reaches the negative saturation level and remains there. \[\boxed{\text{(c) Positive supply voltage }+V_{CC}}\]

The poles of the system are the eigenvalues of the state matrix \(A\). The characteristic equation is: \[|sI-A|=0\] \[\left|
\begin{array}{cc}
s &amp; -1\\
2 &amp; s+3
\end{array}
\right|=0\] \[s^2+3s+2=0\] \[s=-1,\,-2\] \[\boxed{(A)\;-2,\,-1}\]

The transfer function of a PI controller is: \[G_c(s)=K_p\left(1+\frac{1}{T_is}\right)\] Given, \[G_c(s)=2+\frac{0.4}{s}\] Comparing with the standard form, \[K_p=2, \qquad \text{and} \qquad \frac{K_p}{T_i}=0.4\] \[\qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \quad \frac{2}{T_i}=0.4\] \[\qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad T_i=5~\text{min}\] The proportional band is: \(PB=\dfrac{100}{K_p}
=\dfrac{100}{2}
=50\%.\) \[\boxed{(D)\;50\%\ \text{and}\ 5~\text{min.}}\]

The transfer function of the inner loop is \[G_i(s)=\frac{K_1}{s+1+K_1}\] The open loop transfer function of the outer loop is \[G_{eq}(s)=\frac{8K_2}{(s+2)(s+4)}\,G_i(s)
=\frac{8K_1K_2}{(s+2)(s+4)(s+1+K_1)}\] The characteristic equation of the overall closed-loop system is \[1+\frac{8K_1K_2}{(s+2)(s+4)(s+1+K_1)}=0\] \[(s+2)(s+4)(s+1+K_1)+8K_1K_2=0\] \[(s+2)(s+4)\left(\frac{s+1}{K_1}+1\right)+8K_2=0\] As \(K_1\rightarrow\infty,\) \(\dfrac{s+1}{K_1}\rightarrow0.\) Hence, the characteristic equation reduces to, \[(s+2)(s+4)+8K_2=0\] \[s^2+6s+8+8K_2=0\] The Routh array is: \[\begin{array}{c|cc}
s^2 &amp; 1 &amp; 8+8K_2\\
s^1 &amp; 6 &amp; 0\\
s^0 &amp; 8+8K_2 &amp;
\end{array}\] For stability, \[8+8K_2&gt;0\] \[K_2&gt;-1\] Therefore, there is no finite upper limit on either controller gain. \[\boxed{(A)\;\infty,\;\infty}\]

From the given root locus, the system reaches the stability limit when the roots lie on the imaginary axis, that is, \(s=\pm j\sqrt{2}\). The open-loop transfer function is: \[G(s)=\frac{2K}{s(s+1)(s+2)}\] Using the magnitude condition, \[\left|\frac{2K}{s(s+1)(s+2)}\right|=1\] \[K=
\left.
\frac{|s(s+1)(s+2)|}{2}
\right|_{s=j\sqrt2}\] \[K=
\left|
\frac{\sqrt2\,j(1+\sqrt2\,j)(2+\sqrt2\,j)}
{2}
\right|
=3\] \[\boxed{(B)\;3.0}\]

From the given root locus, the system exhibits the maximum non-oscillatory response at the breakaway point \(s=-0.423\). Beyond this point, the closed-loop poles become complex and the response becomes oscillatory. Using the magnitude condition, \[\left|\frac{2K}{s(s+1)(s+2)}\right|=1\] \[K=
\left.
\frac{|s(s+1)(s+2)|}{2}
\right|_{s=-0.423}\] \[K=
\frac{|(-0.423)(0.577)(1.577)|}{2}
\approx0.2\] \[\boxed{(D)\;0.2}\]
